complete(identifier,dict(
 title='Deterministic volume gap',status='source_open',criterion='resources',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''For every fixed locally checkable labeling problem \(\Pi\) on all finite graphs of some fixed maximum degree \(\Delta\), is the following implication true?
\[
\bigl[\exists\text{ correct deterministic VOLUME algorithm }A:\lim_{n\to\infty}V_A(n)/n=0\bigr]
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\bigl[\exists\text{ correct deterministic VOLUME algorithm }B\ \exists C>0\ \forall n\ge1:\ V_B(n)\le C(1+\log^* n)\bigr].
\]
The function \(V_A(n)\) is the largest number of distinct vertices visited to determine one requested output on an allowed input with at most \(n\) vertices. Algorithms know the exact size \(N\) of their input graph, and distinct vertex identifiers lie in \(\{1,\ldots,N^2\}\). The model and input class are defined below.''',
 definitions=r'''The problem \(\Pi\) fixes an integer degree bound \(\Delta\ge2\), an integer verification radius \(r\ge1\), finite nonempty input and output alphabets, and a finite collection of permitted rooted radius-\(r\) neighborhoods. A neighborhood records the graph, its root, vertex input and output labels, degrees and local port numbers. Vertices within graph distance at most \(r\) are shown, with their induced edges and incident degrees. The rule ignores identifiers and graph size and is unchanged under rooted isomorphism preserving labels and ports. An output labeling is legal exactly when each vertex sees a permitted neighborhood.

Instances include every finite nonempty simple undirected graph of maximum degree at most \(\Delta\), with every input labeling and every port numbering, including disconnected graphs. A port numbering assigns, independently at each vertex of degree \(d\), its incident edges bijectively to \(\{1,\ldots,d\}\); port numbers at opposite ends need not agree. There is no global graph-structure promise. A problem with unsatisfiable instances simply has no correct algorithm and cannot witness the premise.

A computation requested at vertex \(v\) starts with that vertex visited and with its identifier, input label, degree and port names, the problem description and exact graph size \(N\). A probe chooses an already visited vertex \(w\) and one of its ports, discovers the neighbor \(u\) reached by that edge, and returns \(u\)'s identifier, input label, degree and local port names. It adds \(u\) to the visited set. Repeated probes are permitted. A program can explore only by following edges from visited vertices: it cannot jump to a vertex merely by guessing its identifier. The returned input does not supply the identities of all unvisited neighbors for free.

Only the number of distinct visited vertices, including the initial vertex, is charged. Intermediate computation and working memory have no time or space bound but must terminate on every input. Each algorithm is one finite deterministic program, uniform over all graph sizes. There is no advice, random tape, preprocessing of the graph, persistent shared state or communication between separately requested output computations. Their answers nevertheless must combine into a valid labeling on every allowed instance and every distinct identifier assignment.

For \(n\ge1\), \(V_A(n)\) maximizes this visit count over all such instances with \(1\le N\le n\), all identifiers and all requested vertices. The exact size known to the algorithm in that maximization is \(N\), not the outer bound \(n\). For a correct terminating algorithm this maximum is finite. The constants and the programs in the implication may depend on the fixed problem \(\Pi\), including its degree bound and verification radius, but not on \(n\).

Define \(\log^*n\) as the number of successive applications of \(\log_2\) needed to reach a value at most one; in particular \(\log^*1=0\). The limit in the premise means that for every real \(\varepsilon>0\), some integer \(n_0\) has \(V_A(n)\le\varepsilon n\) for all \(n\ge n_0\). The all-\(n\) bound in the conclusion is equivalent to an eventual big-O bound after enlarging its constant to handle finitely many small sizes. The claim concerns deterministic connected exploration, not randomized VOLUME algorithms or local computation algorithms allowed to probe distant vertices.''',
 answer_criterion=r'''Give a complete mathematically correct Lean-checked proof of the universal implication or a fixed LCL counterexample. A counterexample must include a correct sublinear-volume algorithm and prove that every correct deterministic algorithm \(B\) fails the iterated-logarithmic bound: for every \(C>0\) and \(n_0\), some \(n\ge n_0\) has \(V_B(n)>C(1+\log^*n)\). Randomized intermediate complexities, bounds for one graph promise, or an unproved assertion that an optimal algorithm exists do not suffice.''',
 source_formulation=dict(text='Question 7.1 asks whether deterministic LCL volume can lie strictly between the iterated-logarithmic and linear scales, and conjectures that it cannot. The card expresses the conjecture as an implication between existence of algorithms, retaining the saved connected-probe, exact-size and polynomial-identifier conventions.',caption='Rosenbaum–Suomela, §7.1 Question 7.1; model §§2.1–2.2.',citation='volume',format='editorial_paraphrase'),
 references=old['references'],
 context_blocks=[block('The cost measures the number of inspected vertices rather than their distance from the query. Exploration can adapt to labels and degrees, but visits must remain connected to the initial vertex.','volume'),block('The July 2026 paper adds randomized intermediate complexity scales and expressly retains the deterministic gap as unresolved. Its constructions do not refute the displayed deterministic implication.','recent')],
 progress=[progress('2020','Question 7.1 proposes that the deterministic interval above the iterated-logarithmic and below the linear scale is empty.','volume'),progress('2026-02-23','The updated locality problem list retains the deterministic intermediate-volume question.','problem'),progress('2026-07-10','The checked new LCL complexity constructions concern randomization; §1.1 explicitly leaves the deterministic gap open.','recent')],
),['Retained the existing universal deterministic gap target and fixed model conventions.','Repaired malformed mathematical prose and defined the exact-size versus at-most-n distinction.','Made correctness across independent requests, local probing and the counterexample quantifiers explicit.','Preserved the individual importance score of 85 and distinguished the July randomized results.'],['Read Rosenbaum–Suomela §§2.1–2.2 and §7.1 Question 7.1 from the cached primary paper.','Read the current Suomela problem list VOLUME section.','Read Peng July 2026 §1.1, including the explicit deterministic open-gap statement and the randomized scope; bounded current searches found no later verified resolution.'],'Source-open in the current locality problem list and explicitly retained as open in Peng’s July 2026 §1.1. New randomized constructions do not settle this deterministic connected-exploration question. The source and bounded later-work review through 18 September 2026 is not an exhaustive certification.',summary=['The VOLUME model counts how many graph vertices are inspected to determine one requested output.','Separate deterministic queries must agree on a globally legal labeling without sharing mutable state.','The question asks whether every fixed locally checkable problem with sublinear volume also has iterated-logarithmic volume.','The graph degree, verification radius and label alphabets are fixed, while graph size and identifiers vary.','Recent constructions fill randomized complexity ranges but leave this deterministic gap unresolved.'],expected_sha256=claim['input_sha256'],claim_token=claim['token'])
